{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>As he had schemed.</b> And not as he had done — from here the Sages said: if they had him executed, they are not executed (Makkot 5a)." "<b>To do to his brother.</b> What does <b>לאחיו</b> teach? It teaches regarding conspiring witnesses against a priest's daughter who is married that they are not executed by burning but by the death penalty of the one who lay with her, which is strangulation, as it says (Leviticus 21) ״הִוא בָאֵשׁ תִּשָּׂרֵף״ — she, and not the one who lay with her. Therefore it says here <b>לאחיו</b>: as he had schemed to do to his brother, and not as he had schemed to do to his sister. But in all other death penalties the verse equated woman to man, and conspiring witnesses against a woman are executed like conspiring witnesses against a man — for example, if they testified about her that she killed a person, that she desecrated the Sabbath, they are executed by her death penalty. For 'his sister' is excluded only here, in a case where the conspiracy can be carried out with the death penalty of the one who lay with her (Sifrei; Sanhedrin 90a)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:44.220739609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}